<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-factoring-and-expansion" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Factoring and Expansion</title>

	<objectives>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				In this section, we learn how to:
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<ol>
			<li><p>Expand products of binomials.</p></li>
			<li><p>Factor expressions into products of binomials</p></li>
    </ol>
	</objectives>

	<subsection>
		<title>Expansion</title>

    <p>
      Before we dig in, let's just point out that the last section had a <emph>lot</emph> of content. Hang in there. I know that it was a lot to take in.
      Don't give up! I encourage you to focus on understanding one new big thing each day. If you want to get good at basketball, you have to practice.
      If you want to become fluent in speaking Japanese, you have to practice. Math is the same.
      You have to practice.
      Some days, it will feel very frustrating to practice. That's okay. Just keep going.
      When you feel confused, find a resource that is helpful and sustainable for getting help and getting your questions answered.
      One of the things that this book tries to do is to provide you with useful sample language. Emulate this language, and when you are confused, see if you can build the words of a question you have
      based on the language you read in this text.
    </p>

    <p>
      In this section, we will look at <emph>products of binomials</emph>, which are expressions of the format <m>(a+b)(c+d)</m>.
      As before, a product means the result of multiplication, and that multiplication happens between the value of <m>(a+b)</m> and the vaule of <m>(c+d)</m>.
      By <term>binomial</term>, we mean <m>(a+b)</m> is a binomial, and <m>(c+d)</m> is a binomial.
      We start with expansion, where we turn this product of binomials <m>(a+b)(c+d)</m> into an expression of the format <m>Ax^2+Bx+C</m>, known as a <term>trinomial</term>.
      Because the highest power is a <m>2</m>, we note that <m>Ax^2+Bx+C</m> is a <term>quadratic</term>. 
      In <m>Ax^2+Bx+C</m>, we will call <m>A</m> the <term>quadratic coefficient</term>, call <m>B</m> the <term>linear coefficient</term>, and call <m>C</m> the <term>constant term</term>.
      For example, in <m>5x^2+6x+7</m>, the quadratic coefficient is <m>5</m>, the linear coefficient is <m>6</m>, and the constant term is <m>7</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      We will start with <m>(a+b)(c+d)</m> where both <m>a</m> and <m>c</m> are the variable <m>x</m>. This will lead to the quadratic coefficient <m>A</m> in <m>Ax^2+Bx+C</m> 
      always being <m>1</m>. In these all such examples, we will start with expressions of the form <m>(a+b)(c+d)</m>, but then look at the expansion <m>Ax^2+Bx+C</m>, 
      comparing the expansions from several examples to see if there are any patterns.
      Spoiler alert: some of the patterns can be come about from examining which coefficients in <m>Ax^2+Bx+C</m> are positive and which coefficients are negative. 
    </p>

    <p>
      Let's take our initial Distributive Law <m>a(b+c) = ab + ac</m>, for the sake of clarity, temporarily replace the letters <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m> 
      with <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> and <m>z</m> instead. It is technically the same formula to have
      <me>x(y+z) = xy + xz</me>
      but in this version of the Distributive Law, we will subsitute <m>x=a+b</m> and <m>y=c</m> and <m>z=d</m> to get
      <me>(a+b)(c+d) = (a+b)c + (a+b)d.</me>
      Note that in the equation above, the left side is <m>(a+b)(c+d)</m> instead of <m>a+b(c+d)</m> due to the Order of Operations: we really needed the value of <m>x</m>, which is <m>a+b</m>,
      to be multiplied by <m>(c+d)</m>. Due to the fact that the Order of Operations says to do multiplication before addition, we need to use parentheses to protect the value of <m>a+b</m>,
      since it is the value of <m>a+b</m> that needs to be multiplied by <m>c+d</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      On the right side of the equation above, we have <m>(a+b)c + (a+b)d</m>, and we can apply the Distributive Law to rewrite <m>(a+b)c</m> as <m>ac + bc</m>, 
      and rewrite <m>(a+b)d</m> as <m>ad + bd</m>. Putting all the pieces together, we have
      <me>(a+b)(c+d) = ac + bc + ad + bd.</me>
    </p>

    <principle xml:id="FOIL1">
      <title>Expanding the Product of Two Binomials</title>
      <p>
        <me>(a+b)(c+d) = ac + ad + bc + bd</me>
      </p>
    </principle>
    
    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Expand and simplify <m>(x+3)(x+5)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We can use <xref ref="FOIL1" /> with <m>a=x</m> and <m>b=3</m> and <m>c=x</m> and <m>d=5</m> to get
          <me>(x+3)(x+5) = x\cdot x + x\cdot 5 + 3\cdot x + 3\cdot 5 = x^2 + 5x + 3x + 15 = x^2 + 8x + 15.</me>
        </p>
      </answer  >
    </example>
    <note>
      <p>
        Because it will be very useful later, we examine the final expression <m>x^2+8x+15</m>
        identifying that it is in the form <m>Ax^2+Bx+C</m>
        by noting the quadratic coefficient is <m>A=1</m>, the linear coefficient <m>B=8</m>, and the constant term is <m>C=15</m>.
        It is very important to note that the linear coefficient <m>8</m> is the sum of the numbers <m>3</m> and <m>5</m> in the original expression,
        while the constant term <m>15</m> is the product of the numbers <m>3</m> and <m>5</m> in the original expression.
      </p>
    </note>
    <p>
      Recall from <xref ref="geometric-addition" /> that addition can be represented by gluing sticks together, and from <xref ref="geometric-multiplication" /> that multiplication can be represented by area of a rectangle.
      So we can represent <m>(x+3)(x+5)=x^2 + 8x + 15</m> through the following diagram:
      <figure>
        <caption>Picture of <m>(x+3)(x+5) = x^2 + 8x + 15</m></caption>
        <image width="30%">
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                \node at (1,2.25) {$x^2$};
                \node at (3,0.75) {$15$};
                \node at (3, 2.25) {$5x$};
                \node at (1,0.75) {$3x$};
                \node at (-.5,2.25) {$x$};
                \node at (1,3.25) {$x$};
                \node at (-.5,0.75) {$3$};
                \node at (3,3.25) {$5$};
              \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
      Note the figure is not to scale.
      Lengths of sides are labeled right next to the side.
      Areas of rectangles are written in the middle of each rectangle.
      We filled in the areas <m>5x</m> and <m>3x</m> first, and then it was natural to fill in the side lengths <m>5</m> and <m>3</m> after.
    </p>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Expand and simplify <m>(x+4)(x+10)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We can use <xref ref="FOIL1" /> with <m>a=x</m> and <m>b=4</m> and <m>c=x</m> and <m>d=10</m> to get
          <me>(x+4)(x+10) = x\cdot x + x\cdot 10 + 4\cdot x + 4\cdot 10 = x^2 + 10x + 4x + 40 = x^2 + 14x + 40.</me>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>
    <note>
      <p>
        Because it will be very useful later, we examine the final expression <m>x^2+14x+40</m>
        identifying that it is in the form <m>Ax^2+Bx+C</m>
        by noting the quadratic coefficient is <m>A=1</m>, the linear coefficient <m>B=14</m>, and the constant term is <m>C=40</m>.
        It is very important to note that the linear coefficient <m>14</m> is the sum of the numbers <m>4</m> and <m>10</m> in the original expression,
        while the constant term <m>40</m> is the product of the numbers <m>4</m> and <m>10</m> in the original expression.
      </p>
    </note>
    <p>
      We can represent <m>(x+4)(x+10)=x^2 + 10x +4x + 40 = x^2 + 14x + 40</m> through the following diagram:
      <figure>
        <caption>Picture of <m>(x+4)(x+10) = x^2 + 10x + 4x + 40</m></caption>
        <image width="25%">
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                \node at (1,2.25) {$x^2$};
                \node at (3,0.75) {$40$};
                \node at (3, 2.25) {$10x$};
                \node at (1,0.75) {$4x$};
                \node at (-.5,2.25) {$x$};
                \node at (1,3.25) {$x$};
                \node at (-.5,0.75) {$4$};
                \node at (3,3.25) {$10$};
              \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
      Like before, the figure is not to scale.
    </p>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Expand and simplify <m>(x+5)(x+5)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>(x+5)(x+5) = x^2 + 5x + 5x + 25 = x^2 + 10x + 25.</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>
    <p>
      We can represent <m>(x+5)(x+5)=x^2 + 5x +5x + 25 = x^2 + 10x + 25</m> through the following diagram:
      <figure>
        <caption>Picture of <m>(x+5)(x+5) = x^2 + 5x + 5x + 25</m></caption>
        <image width="25%">
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                \node at (1,2.25) {$x^2$};
                \node at (3,0.75) {$25$};
                \node at (3, 2.25) {$5x$};
                \node at (1,0.75) {$5x$};
                \node at (-.5,2.25) {$x$};
                \node at (1,3.25) {$x$};
                \node at (-.5,0.75) {$5$};
                \node at (3,3.25) {$5$};
              \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </p>
    <warning>
      <p>
        Given the expression <m>(x+8)^2</m> this is not <m>x^2+64</m>. For more information, see <xref ref="warning-exponents-dont-distribute" />.
      </p>
    </warning>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Expand and simplify <m>(x+8)^2</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>(x+8)^2 = (x+8)(x+8) = x^2 + 8x + 8x + 64 = x^2 + 16x + 64.</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>
    <p>
      We can represent <m>(x+8)(x+8)=x^2 + 8x + 8x + 64 = x^2 + 16x + 64</m> through the following diagram:
      <figure>
        <caption>Picture of <m>(x+8)(x+8) = x^2 + 8x + 8x + 64</m></caption>
        <image width="25%">
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                \node at (1,2.25) {$x^2$};
                \node at (3,0.75) {$64$};
                \node at (3, 2.25) {$8x$};
                \node at (1,0.75) {$8x$};
                \node at (-.5,2.25) {$x$};
                \node at (1,3.25) {$x$};
                \node at (-.5,0.75) {$8$};
                \node at (3,3.25) {$8$};
              \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </p>

    <example xml:id="preview-factor-by-grouping">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Expand and simplify <m>(x^2+2)(x+5)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <m>(x^2+2)(x+5) = x^3 + 5x^2 + 2x + 10.</m>
      </answer>
    </example>
    <p>
      This example is a little different in that the first term in the first factor is <m>x^2</m> instead of <m>x</m>. Nevertheless, <xref ref="FOIL1" /> still applies.
      Once we applied this expansion, there were no like terms to combine, thus no further simplification to be done.
    </p>

    <principle xml:id="FOIL2">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <me>(a-b)(c-d) = ac - ad - bc + bd</me>          
        </p>
      </statement>
    </principle>
    <p>
      Whether you choose to use this version <m>(a-b)(c-d) = ac - ad - bc + bd</m> directly or think via <xref ref="FOIL1" /> is a matter of personal 
      choice. Let's demonstrate the two ways to think about this through an example.
    </p>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Expand and simplify <m>(x-3)(x-8)</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          The given expression <m>(x-3)(x-8)</m> fits the format of the left side <m>(a-b)(c-d)</m> in <xref ref="FOIL2" />
          with <m>a=x</m> and <m>b=3</m> and <m>c=x</m> and <m>d=8</m>.
          In doing these identifications, the confusion may be why the stating of <m>b</m> and <m>d</m> do not have minus signs,
          but this is because the format of <m>(a-b)(c-d)</m> already includes minus signs. Then, by <xref ref="FOIL2" />,
          <me>(x-3)(x-8) = x \cdot x - x \cdot 8 - 3 \cdot x + 3 \cdot 8 = x^2 - 8x - 3x + 24 = x^2 - 11x + 24.</me>
        </p>
      </answer>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We can rewrite the expression <m>(x-3)(x-8) = (x+(-3))(x+(-8))</m> because <m>(x+(-3))(x+(-8))</m> fits the format of the left
          side <m>(a+b)(c+d)</m> in <xref ref="FOIL1" />. In this format, <m>a=x</m>, and <m>b</m> should consist of content right after the first plus sign,
          so <m>b=-3</m>. Similarly, <m>c=x</m>, and <m>d</m> should be all the content after the second plus sign, so <m>d=-8</m>. Then by <xref ref="FOIL1" />,
          <me>(x+(-3))(x+(-8)) = x \cdot x + x \cdot (-8) + (-3) \cdot x + (-3)(-8) = x^2 - 8x - 3x + 24 = x^2-11x+24.</me>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>
    <note>
      <p>
        Because it will be very useful later, we examine the final expression <m>x^2-11x+24</m>
        identifying that it is in the form <m>Ax^2+Bx+C</m> will need us to first slightly rewrite the final expression as <m>x^2 + (-11)x + 24</m>.
        by noting the quadratic coefficient is <m>A=1</m>, the linear coefficient <m>B=-11</m>, and the constant term is <m>C=24</m>.
        It is very important to note that the linear coefficient <m>-11</m> is the sum of the numbers <m>-3</m> and <m>-8</m> in the original expression,
        while the constant term <m>24</m> is the product of the numbers <m>-3</m> and <m>-8</m> in the original expression.
        (While we noted <m>(-3)+(-8)=-11</m> we can also note <m>3+8=11</m>.)
      </p>
    </note>
    <p>
      We can represent <m>(x-3)(x-8)= x^2 - 8x - 3x + 24 = x^2 - 11x + 24</m> through the following diagram:
      <figure>
        <caption>Picture of <m>(x-3)(x-8) = x^2 - 8x - 3x + 24</m></caption>
        <image width="25%">
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                \node at (1,2.25) {$x^2$};
                \node at (3,0.75) {$24$};
                \node at (3, 2.25) {$-8x$};
                \node at (1,0.75) {$-3x$};
                \node at (-.5,2.25) {$x$};
                \node at (1,3.25) {$x$};
                \node at (-.5,0.75) {$-3$};
                \node at (3,3.25) {$-8$};
              \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
      It definitely feels a little strange to label the length of a stick by a negative number,
      but everything still works:
      Vertically, we have <m>x + (-3) = x- 3</m>.
      Horizontally, we have <m>x + (-8) = x - 8</m>.
      The four small rectangles are labeled with their areas, namely, 
      <m>x \cdot x = x^2</m> and <m>x \cdot -8 = -8x</m> and <m>-3 \cdot x = -3x</m> and <m>(-3)(-8) = 24</m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Before moving on to another example, 
      we note it is a matter of personal taste whether there really was a need to introduce a separate formula as <xref ref="FOIL2" />.
      You may prefer to apply principles such as a negative times a negative results in a positive, without needing to introduce a variant of <xref ref="FOIL1" />.
    </p>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Expand and simplify <m>(x-4)(x-6)</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          The given expression <m>(x-4)(x-6)</m> fits the format of the left side <m>(a-b)(c-d)</m> in <xref ref="FOIL2" />
          with <m>a=x</m> and <m>b=4</m> and <m>c=x</m> and <m>d=6</m>.
          In doing these identifications, the confusion may be why the stating of <m>b</m> and <m>d</m> do not have minus signs,
          but this is because the format of <m>(a-b)(c-d)</m> already includes minus signs. Then, by <xref ref="FOIL2" />,
          <me>(x-4)(x-6) = x \cdot x - x \cdot 6 - 4 \cdot x + 4 \cdot 6 = x^2 - 6x - 4x + 24 = x^2 - 10x + 24.</me>
        </p>
      </answer>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We can rewrite the expression <m>(x-4)(x-6) = (x+(-4))(x+(-6))</m> because <m>(x+(-4))(x+(-6))</m> fits the format of the left
          side <m>(a+b)(c+d)</m> in <xref ref="FOIL1" />. In this format, <m>a=x</m>, and <m>b</m> should consist of content right after the first plus sign,
          so <m>b=-4</m>. Similarly, <m>c=x</m>, and <m>d</m> should be all the content after the second plus sign, so <m>d=-6</m>. Then by <xref ref="FOIL1" />,
          <me>(x+(-4))(x+(-6)) = x \cdot x + x \cdot (-6) + (-4) \cdot x + (-4)(-6) = x^2 - 6x - 4x + 24 = x^2-10x+24.</me>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>
    <note>
      <p>
        In <m>x^2-10x+24</m>, we can rewrite the final expression as <m>x^2 + (-10)x + 24</m>, which has linear coefficient <m>-10</m> and constant term <m>24</m>.
        Note that the constant <m>24</m> is the product of the numbers <m>(-4)</m> and <m>(-6)</m> in the original expression
        (or we can point out that the product of the numbers after the minus signs, namely <m>4</m> and <m>6</m> multiply to <m>24</m>).
        Thus the linear coefficient <m>-10</m> is the sum of the numbers <m>-4</m> and <m>-6</m>, (or we can point out that the numbers after the minus signs <m>4</m> and <m>6</m> add to <m>10</m>, the number after the minus sign in <m>x^2-10x+24</m>).
      </p>
    </note>
    <p>
      We can represent <m>(x-4)(x-6)= x^2 - 6x - 4x + 24 = x^2 - 10x + 24</m> through the following diagram:
      <figure>
        <caption>Picture of <m>(x-4)(x-6) = x^2 - 6x - 4x + 24</m></caption>
        <image width="25%">
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                \node at (1,2.25) {$x^2$};
                \node at (3,0.75) {$24$};
                \node at (3, 2.25) {$-6x$};
                \node at (1,0.75) {$-4x$};
                \node at (-.5,2.25) {$x$};
                \node at (1,3.25) {$x$};
                \node at (-.5,0.75) {$-4$};
                \node at (3,3.25) {$-6$};
              \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </p>




    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Expand and simplify <m>(x-5)(x-5)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>(x-5)(x-5) = x^2 - 5x - 5x + 25 = x^2 - 10x + 25.</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <p>
      We can represent <m>(x-5)(x-5)=x^2 - 5x - 5x + 25 = x^2 - 10x + 25</m> through the following diagram:
      <figure>
        <caption>Picture of <m>(x-5)(x-5) = x^2 - 5x - 5x + 25</m></caption>
        <image width="25%">
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                \node at (1,2.25) {$x^2$};
                \node at (3,0.75) {$25$};
                \node at (3, 2.25) {$-5x$};
                \node at (1,0.75) {$-5x$};
                \node at (-.5,2.25) {$x$};
                \node at (1,3.25) {$x$};
                \node at (-.5,0.75) {$-5$};
                \node at (3,3.25) {$-5$};
              \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </p>

    
    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Expand and simplify <m>(x-8)^2</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>(x-8)^2=(x-8)(x-8) = x^2 - 8x - 8x + 64 = x^2 - 16x + 64.</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>
    <p>
      We can represent <m>(x-8)(x-8)=x^2 - 8x - 8x + 64 = x^2 - 16x + 64</m> through the following diagram:
      <figure>
        <caption>Picture of <m>(x-8)(x-8) = x^2 - 8x - 8x + 64</m></caption>
        <image width="25%">
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                \node at (1,2.25) {$x^2$};
                \node at (3,0.75) {$64$};
                \node at (3, 2.25) {$-8x$};
                \node at (1,0.75) {$-8x$};
                \node at (-.5,2.25) {$x$};
                \node at (1,3.25) {$x$};
                \node at (-.5,0.75) {$-8$};
                \node at (3,3.25) {$-8$};
              \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </p>


    <p>
      We present two formulas together because they are similar: in each, the left side is a product of two binomials, with one binomial having a plus sign in between, and one binomial having a minus sign in between.
    </p>
    <principle xml:id="principle-FOIL3">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>(a+b)(c-d) = ac - ad + bc - bd</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>(a-b)(c+d) = ac + ad - bc - bd</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </principle>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Expand and simplify <m>(x+7)(x-2)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We can use the first formula in <xref ref="principle-FOIL3" /> with <m>a=x</m> and <m>b=7</m> and <m>c=x</m> and <m>d=2</m> to get
          <me>(x+7)(x-2) = x\cdot x - x\cdot 2 + 7\cdot x - 7\cdot 2 = x^2 - 2x + 7x - 14 = x^2 + 5x - 14.</me>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>
    <note>
      <p>
        For the first time in this section, we end with a quadratic expression <m>x^2 + 5x - 14</m> where the constant term is negative.
        This happened because we multiplied <m>+7</m> with <m>-2</m>.
        Recall the product of a real numbers with opposite signs results in a negative number.
        In the original expression, let's examine the <m>7</m> and the <m>2</m> which appear after the plus sign and the minus sign.
        The difference between these numbers is <m>5</m>, and note the linear coefficient was <m>5</m>.
      </p>
    </note>
    <p>
      We can represent <m>(x+7)(x-2)=x^2 - 2x + 7x - 14 = x^2 + 5x - 14</m> through the following diagram:
      <figure>
        <caption>Picture of <m>(x+7)(x-2) = x^2 -2x + 7x - 14</m></caption>
        <image width="25%">
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                \node at (1,2.25) {$x^2$};
                \node at (3,0.75) {$-14$};
                \node at (3, 2.25) {$7x$};
                \node at (1,0.75) {$-2x$};
                \node at (-.5,2.25) {$x$};
                \node at (1,3.25) {$x$};
                \node at (-.5,0.75) {$7$};
                \node at (3,3.25) {$-2$};
              \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
      The vertical length is <m>x+7</m> and the horizontal length is <m>x+(-2)=x-2</m>.
      The four small rectangles are labeled with their areas, namely,
      <m>x \cdot x = x^2</m> and <m>x \cdot -2 = -2x</m> and <m>7 \cdot x = 7x</m> and <m>7 \cdot -2 = -14</m>
    </p>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Expand and simplify <m>(x-7)(x+2)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We can use the second formula in <xref ref="principle-FOIL3" /> with <m>a=x</m> and <m>b=7</m> and <m>c=x</m> and <m>d=2</m> to get
          <me>(x-7)(x+2) = x\cdot x + x\cdot 2 - 7\cdot x - 7\cdot 2 = x^2 + 2x - 7x - 14 = x^2 - 5x - 14.</me>
        </p>
      </answer>      
    </example>
    <note>
      <p>
        As in our previous example, the final expression <m>x^2 - 5x - 14</m> has a negative constant term, for the same reason as earlier:
        this was the result of multiplying a negative number (namely <m>-7</m>) together with a positive number (namely <m>+2</m>).
        The opposite of the linear coefficient <m>-5</m> is <m>5</m>, and that is the difference between the numbers <m>7</m> and <m>2</m> in the original expression.
        When we subtract <m>2</m> and <m>7</m> in a certain order, we get <m>-5</m>.         
      </p>
    </note>
    <p>
      We can represent <m>(x-7)(x+2)=x^2 + 2x - 7x - 14 = x^2 - 5x - 14</m> through the following diagram:
      <figure>
        <caption>Picture of <m>(x-7)(x+2) = x^2 - 5x - 14</m></caption>
        <image width="25%">
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                \node at (1,2.25) {$x^2$};
                \node at (3,0.75) {$-14$};
                \node at (3, 2.25) {$2x$};
                \node at (1,0.75) {$-7x$};
                \node at (-.5,2.25) {$x$};
                \node at (1,3.25) {$x$};
                \node at (-.5,0.75) {$-7$};
                \node at (3,3.25) {$2$};
              \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
      The vertical length is <m>x+(-7)=x-7</m> and the horizontal length is <m>x+2</m>.
      The four small rectangles are labeled with their areas, namely,
      <m>x \cdot x = x^2</m> and <m>x \cdot -2 = -2x</m> and <m>-7 \cdot x = -7x</m> and <m>-7 \cdot -2 = 14</m>
    </p>


    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Expand and simplify <m>(x-3)(x+10)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>(x-3)(x+10) = x^2 + 10x - 3x - 30 = x^2 + 7x - 30</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>
    <p>
      We can represent <m>(x-3)(x+10)=x^2 + 10x - 3x - 30 = x^2 + 7x - 30</m> through the following diagram:
      <figure>
        <caption>Picture of <m>(x-3)(x+10) = x^2 + 7x - 30</m></caption>
        <image width="25%">
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                \node at (1,2.25) {$x^2$};
                \node at (3,0.75) {$-30$};
                \node at (3, 2.25) {$10x$};
                \node at (1,0.75) {$-3x$};
                \node at (-.5,2.25) {$x$};
                \node at (1,3.25) {$x$};
                \node at (-.5,0.75) {$-3$};
                \node at (3,3.25) {$10$};
              \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </p>
    

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Expand and simplify <m>(x+9)(x-9)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>(x+9)(x-9) = x^2 - 9x + 9x - 81 = x^2 + 0x - 81 = x^2 - 81</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>
    <note>
      <p>
        We have been looking at expanding and simplifying the product of binomials.
        Typically in these questions, the expansion creates four terms and then collecting of like terms leads to a total of three terms.
        In this case, we only have two terms in <m>x^2-81</m> because the middle terms <m>-9x+9x</m> simplified to zero.
      </p>
    </note>
    <p>
      We can represent <m>(x+9)(x-9)= x^2 - 9x + 9x - 81 = x^2 + 0x - 81 = x^2 - 81</m> through the following diagram:
      <figure>
        <caption>Picture of <m>(x+9)(x-9) = x^2 - 81</m></caption>
        <image width="25%">
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                \node at (1,2.25) {$x^2$};
                \node at (3,0.75) {$-81$};
                \node at (3, 2.25) {$-9x$};
                \node at (1,0.75) {$9x$};
                \node at (-.5,2.25) {$x$};
                \node at (1,3.25) {$x$};
                \node at (-.5,0.75) {$9$};
                \node at (3,3.25) {$-9$};
              \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </p>
    
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Factoring</title>

    <p>
      Our goal here is to take a trinomial in the format of <m>Ax^2+Bx+C</m> such as <m>x^2+7x-30</m> and <m>x^2+14x+40</m> and factor this,
      producing a product of binomials. There are various techniques, and it is advisable to understand every techinque introduced, not just one technique.
      We will point out the connections between the techniques: that is, we point out what questions get asked in various ways but actually require us to do the same thinking.
      Some of the factoring techniques are ideal in certain situations, while some of the techniques work in more general settings.
    </p>
    <p>
      We start with the situation where <m>A=1</m>. That is, we will focus on factoring a quadratic like <m>2x^2+11x-40</m> a little later, since the quadratic coefficient <m>A=2</m>,
      and for now instead focus on a quadratic such as <m>x^2+7x-30</m>, where the quadratic coefficient <m>A=1</m>, seen more clearly when rewritten as <m>1x^2+7x-30</m>.
      We mentioned that some techniques only work in certain situations.
      To be specific, some of the techniques that we'll see only apply when <m>A=1</m>.
      Let's start digging into techniques. It is helpful to see what happens based on whether the constant term is positive or negative.
    </p>

    <!-- Try it -->
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Compare the examples in the previous portion on expansion.
          What patterns do you notice about the constant term <m>C</m> in the final expression <m>x^2+Bx+C</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <!-- <response /> --><!-- Commented out the response area where a student can type. -->
    </exercise>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Will the two binomials in the factored form of <m>x^2+8x+15</m> have the same sign or opposite signs?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          Because the constant term is positive (namely <m>15</m>), the two binomials will have the same sign.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Will the two binomials in the factored form of <m>x^2+8x-20</m> have the same sign or opposite signs?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          Because the constant term is negative (namely <m>-20</m>), the two binomials will have opposite signs.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

    <p>
      We introduce a technique that is known as the Diamond Method, starting with a version that applies when <m>A=1</m>.
    </p>
    <computation xml:id="diamond-method-basic">
      <p>
        To factor <m>x^2+Bx+C</m>, draw the following diagram:
        <figure>
          <caption>Initial diamond figure</caption>
          <image width="15%">
            <latex-image>
              <![CDATA[
                \begin{tikzpicture}
                  \draw (-1,-1) -- (1,1);
                  \draw (-1,1) -- (1,-1);
                  \node at (0,.8) {$C$};
                  \node at (0,-.8) {$B$};
                  \node at (-.8, 0) {};
                  \node at (.8, 0) {};
                \end{tikzpicture}
              ]]>
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
        The remaining two spaces (on the left and right) need to be filled with two numbers that multiply to the number <m>C</m> at the top of the diamond, and add to <m>B</m> at the bottom of the diamond.
        It is often helpful to write all the factor pairs of <m>C</m>, and then go through the list to see which pair of numbers adds to <m>B</m>.
      </p>
    </computation>

    <p>
      Before practicing this technique on examples, we introduce one more technique (though this really is displaying the same information in a different visual format).
      This technique practices the geometric interpretations of addition and multiplication.
    </p>

    <computation xml:id="geometric-factoring-basic">
      <p>
        Recall from <xref ref="geometric-addition" /> that addition can be represented by gluing sticks together, and from <xref ref="geometric-multiplication" /> that multiplication can be represented by area of a rectangle.
        To factor <m>x^2+Bx+C</m>, draw a rectangle and divide it into four smaller rectangles.
        Label the first side vertically and the first side horizontally with <m>x</m>.
        Label the top left rectangle with <m>x^2</m> and the bottom right rectangle with <m>C</m>.
        The second side vertically and the second side horizontally need to be filled in with numbers so that the two numbers multiply to <m>C</m> and the two unlabeled areas add up to <m>Bx</m>.
        <figure>
          <caption>Initial area model figure</caption>
          <image width="25%">
            <latex-image>
              <![CDATA[
                \begin{tikzpicture}
                  \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                  \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                  \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                  \node at (1,2.25) {$x^2$};
                  \node at (3,0.75) {$C$};
                  \node at (3, 2.25) {}; % upper right area
                  \node at (1,0.75) {}; % lower left area
                  %
                  \node at (-.5,2.25) {$x$};
                  \node at (1,3.25) {$x$};
                  \node at (-.5,0.75) {}; % 2nd vertical side
                  \node at (3,3.25) {}; % 2nd horizontal side
                \end{tikzpicture}
              ]]>
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
        While our description above the figure is accurate, it is easier to do repackage it by saying
        that we are looking for two expressions to multiply to <m>Cx^2</m> and add to <m>Bx</m>.
        Here's a practical way to do this:
        Consider the expression <m>C \cdot x^2</m>.
        Write down all pairs of factors that multiply to <m>C \cdot x^2</m>, 
        with an <m>x</m> appearing in each factor.
        Then, among this list of pairs, find the pair that adds to <m>Bx</m>.
        It is often helpful to write all the factor pairs of <m>C</m>, and then go through the list to see which pair of numbers adds to <m>B</m>.
      </p>
    </computation>

    <example xml:id="factor-xsquaredplus11xplus24">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>x^2+11x+24</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We are looking to fill in:
          <figure>
            <caption>Initial diamond figure</caption>
            <image width="15%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (-1,-1) -- (1,1); %
                    \draw (-1,1) -- (1,-1);
                    \node at (0,.8) {$24$};
                    \node at (0,-.8) {$11$};
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          The two numbers must multiply to <m>24</m> and add to <m>11</m>.
          We list the pairs of numbers whose product is <m>24</m>:
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>1</m> and <m>24</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>2</m> and <m>12</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>3</m> and <m>8</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>4</m> and <m>6</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          Then, look through the list to see which pair of numbers adds up to <m>11</m>.
          These are <m>3</m> and <m>8</m>, so we can fill in the diamond figure as follows:
          <figure>
            <caption>Completed diamond figure</caption>
            <image width="15%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (-1,-1) -- (1,1);
                    \draw (-1,1) -- (1,-1);
                    \node at (0,.8) {$24$};
                    \node at (0,-.8) {$11$};
                    \node at (-.8, 0) {$3$};
                    \node at (.8, 0) {$8$};
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          Thus <m>x^2+11x+24= (x+3)(x+8)</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We are looking to fill in the two empty sides in:
          <figure>
            <caption>Initial area model figure</caption>
            <image width="25%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                    \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                    \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                    \node at (1,2.25) {$x^2$};
                    \node at (3,0.75) {$24$};
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          We consider the product of the first term and last term, <m>24 \cdot x^2</m>. 
          Let's list all pairs of factors of <m>24x^2</m> where each factor contains an <m>x</m>:
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>1x</m> and <m>24x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>2x</m> and <m>12x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>3x</m> and <m>8x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>4x</m> and <m>6x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          Then, look through the list to find which pair adds up to <m>11x</m>.
          These are <m>3x</m> and <m>8x</m>, so we can fill in the area model figure as follows:
          <figure>
            <caption>Completed area model figure</caption>
            <image width="30%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                    \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                    \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                    \node at (1,2.25) {$x^2$};
                    \node at (3,0.75) {$24$};
                    \node at (3, 2.25) {$8x$}; % upper right area
                    \node at (1,0.75) {$3x$}; % lower left area
                    %
                    \node at (-.5,2.25) {$x$};
                    \node at (1,3.25) {$x$};
                    \node at (-.5,0.75) {$3$}; % 2nd vertical side
                    \node at (3,3.25) {$8$}; % 2nd horizontal side
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          We filled in the areas <m>3x</m> and <m>8x</m> first. Then the sides <m>3</m> and <m>8</m> were filled in.
          Thus <m>x^2+11x+24= (x+3)(x+8)</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>
    <p>
      Notice that both techniques involve finding two numbers that multiply to <m>24</m> and add to <m>11</m>,
      though in the geometric solution, we were looking for two expressions that multiply to <m>24x^2</m> and add to <m>11x</m>.
    </p>

    <warning xml:id="warning-factor-only">
      <p>
        When the task is to factor, just factor. Do not factor followed by setting the expression equal to zero.
        There is a process we will see later where factoring is the first step.
      </p>      
      <p>
        In the previous question, we are just asked to factor the <emph>expression</emph> <m>x^2+11x+24</m>.
        In a future section, we will be asked to solve the <emph>equation</emph> <m>x^2+11x+24=0</m>, and in that situation,
        one way to do this is to factor the left side, leading to the equation <m>(x+3)(x+8)=0</m>, and then split this into the two equations
        <m>x+3=0</m> or <m>x+8=0</m>, then solve each of the smaller equations: <m>x=-3</m> or <m>x=-8</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        But for now, we are just factoring the expression.
      </p>
    </warning>



    <example xml:id="factor-xsquaredminus12xplus35">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>x^2-12x+35</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We are looking to fill in:
          <figure>
            <caption>Initial diamond figure</caption>
            <image width="15%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (-1,-1) -- (1,1);
                    \draw (-1,1) -- (1,-1);
                    \node at (0,.8) {$35$};
                    \node at (0,-.8) {$-12$};
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          The two numbers must multiply to <m>35</m> and add to <m>-12</m>.
          We list the pairs of numbers whose product is <m>35</m>, but with the following twist.
          Note that the sum of the two numbers needs to be negative.
          So each number must be negative. 
          (It still works to have each pair of numbers multiplying to positive <m>35</m>, since a negative times a negative is a positive.)
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-1</m> and <m>-35</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-5</m> and <m>-7</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          Then, look through the list to see which pair of numbers adds up to <m>-12</m>.
          These are <m>-5</m> and <m>-7</m>, so we can fill in the diamond figure as follows:
          <figure>
            <caption>Completed diamond figure</caption>
            <image width="15%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (-1,-1) -- (1,1);
                    \draw (-1,1) -- (1,-1);
                    \node at (0,.8) {$35$};
                    \node at (0,-.8) {$-12$};
                    \node at (-.8, 0) {$-5$};
                    \node at (.8, 0) {$-7$};
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          Thus <m>x^2-12x+35= (x-5)(x-7)</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We are looking to fill in the two empty sides in:
          <figure>
            <caption>Initial area model figure</caption>
            <image width="25%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                    \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                    \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                    \node at (1,2.25) {$x^2$};
                    \node at (3,0.75) {$35$};
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          We consider the product of the first term and last term, <m>35 \cdot x^2</m>. 
          Let's list all pairs of factors of <m>35x^2</m> where each factor contains an <m>x</m>,
          and each factor is written with a minus sign, because we'll need the sum to be <m>-12x</m>.
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-1x</m> and <m>-35x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-5x</m> and <m>-7x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          Then, look through the list to find the pair adds up to <m>-12x</m>.
          These are <m>-5x</m> and <m>-7x</m>, so we can fill in the area model figure as follows:
          <figure>
            <caption>Completed area model figure</caption>
            <image width="25%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                    \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                    \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                    \node at (1,2.25) {$x^2$};
                    \node at (3,0.75) {$35$};
                    \node at (3, 2.25) {$-7x$}; % upper right area
                    \node at (1,0.75) {$-5x$}; % lower left area
                    %
                    \node at (-.5,2.25) {$x$};
                    \node at (1,3.25) {$x$};
                    \node at (-.5,0.75) {$-5$}; % 2nd vertical side
                    \node at (3,3.25) {$-7$}; % 2nd horizontal side
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          We filled in the areas <m>-5x</m> and <m>-7x</m> first. Then the sides <m>-5</m> and <m>-7</m> were filled in.
          Thus <m>x^2-12x+35= (x-5)(x-7)</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>
    <p>
      Notice that both techniques involve finding two numbers that multiply to <m>35</m> and add to <m>-12</m>,
      though in the geometric solution, we were looking for two expressions that multiply to <m>35x^2</m> and add to <m>-12x</m>.
    </p>


    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>x^2 + 5x - 14</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We are looking to fill in:
          <figure>
            <caption>Initial diamond figure</caption>
            <image width="15%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (-1,-1) -- (1,1);
                    \draw (-1,1) -- (1,-1);
                    \node at (0,.8) {$-14$};
                    \node at (0,-.8) {$5$};
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          The two numbers must multiply to <m>-14</m> and add to <m>5</m>.
          We list the pairs of numbers whose product is <m>-14</m>:
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>1</m> and <m>-14</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-1</m> and <m>14</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>2</m> and <m>-7</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-2</m> and <m>7</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          Then, look through the list to find which pair of numbers adds up to <m>5</m>.
          These are <m>-2</m> and <m>7</m>. 
          (In the list above, since the product has to be negative, we have two numbers of opposite signs.
          In the future, we could have taken a bit of a shortcut in writing the list above by only writing pairs where the bigger number is positive,
          since we know the sum is positive.)
          We can fill in the diamond figure as follows:
          <figure>
            <caption>Completed diamond figure</caption>
            <image width="15%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (-1,-1) -- (1,1);
                    \draw (-1,1) -- (1,-1);
                    \node at (0,.8) {$-14$};
                    \node at (0,-.8) {$5$};
                    \node at (-.8, 0) {$-2$};
                    \node at (.8, 0) {$7$};
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          Thus <m>x^2+5x-14= (x-2)(x+7)</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We are looking to fill in the two empty sides in:
          <figure>
            <caption>Initial area model figure</caption>
            <image width="25%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                    \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                    \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                    \node at (1,2.25) {$x^2$};
                    \node at (3,0.75) {$-14$};
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          We consider the product of the first term and last term, <m>-14 \cdot x^2</m>. 
          Let's list all pairs of factors of <m>-14x^2</m> where each factor contains an <m>x</m>,
          but knowing that the sum needs to be positive, namely positive <m>5x</m>, we know the bigger factor must be positive and the smaller factor must be negative.
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-1x</m> and <m>+14x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-2x</m> and <m>7x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          We did not list the pair <m>1x</m> and <m>-14x</m>, and we also didn't list the pair <m>2x</m> and <m>-7x</m>, 
          because the sum needs to be <m>5x</m> which has a positive coefficient, so we know that we need more
          <q>positive <m>x</m></q> than <q>negative <m>x</m></q>.
          Then, look through the list to see which pair adds up to <m>5x</m>.
          These are <m>-2x</m> and <m>7x</m>, so we can fill in the area model figure as follows:
          <figure>
            <caption>Completed area model figure</caption>
            <image width="30%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                    \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                    \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                    \node at (1,2.25) {$x^2$};
                    \node at (3,0.75) {$-14$};
                    \node at (3, 2.25) {$7x$}; % upper right area
                    \node at (1,0.75) {$-2$}; % lower left area
                    %
                    \node at (-.5,2.25) {$x$};
                    \node at (1,3.25) {$x$};
                    \node at (-.5,0.75) {$-2$}; % 2nd vertical side
                    \node at (3,3.25) {$7$}; % 2nd horizontal side
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          We filled in <m>7x</m> and <m>-2x</m> first, and then the side lengths <m>7</m> and <m>-2</m> after.
          Thus <m>x^2+5x-14 = (x-2)(x+7)</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>
    <p>
      Notice that both techniques involve finding two numbers that multiply to <m>-14</m> and add to <m>5</m>,
      though in the geometric solution, we were looking for two expressions that multiply to <m>-14x^2</m> and add to <m>5x</m>.
    </p>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>x^2 - 10x - 24</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We are looking to fill in:
          <figure>
            <caption>Initial diamond figure</caption>
            <image width="15%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (-1,-1) -- (1,1);
                    \draw (-1,1) -- (1,-1);
                    \node at (0,.8) {$-24$};
                    \node at (0,-.8) {$-10$};
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          The two numbers must multiply to <m>-24</m> and add to <m>-10</m>.
          We list the pairs of numbers whose product is <m>-24</m>,
          meaning that there must be one positive number and one negative number in each pair,
          but knowing that the sum needs to be negative, namely negative <m>-10</m>,
          we know the bigger factor must be negative and the smaller factor must be positive.
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>1</m> and <m>-24</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>2</m> and <m>-12</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>3</m> and <m>-8</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>4</m> and <m>-6</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          Then, look through the list to see which pair of numbers adds up to <m>-10</m>.
          These are <m>2</m> and <m>-12</m>, so we can fill in the diamond figure as follows:
          <figure>
            <caption>Completed diamond figure</caption>
            <image width="15%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (-1,-1) -- (1,1);
                    \draw (-1,1) -- (1,-1);
                    \node at (0,.8) {$-24$};
                    \node at (0,-.8) {$-10$};
                    \node at (-.8, 0) {$-12$};
                    \node at (.8, 0) {$2$};
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          Thus <m>x^2-10x-24= (x-12)(x+2)</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
      <answer>
        <p>
          Here is our initial diagram:
          <figure>
            <caption>Initial area model figure</caption>
            <image width="25%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                    \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                    \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                    \node at (1,2.25) {$x^2$};
                    \node at (3,0.75) {$-24$};
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          We consider the product of the first term and last term, <m>-24 \cdot x^2</m>. 
          Let's list all pairs of factors of <m>-24x^2</m> where each factor contains an <m>x</m>,
          but knowing that the sum needs to be <m>-10x</m>, we know the bigger factor must have a negative coefficient
          and the smaller factor have a positive coefficient.
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>1x</m> and <m>-24x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>2x</m> and <m>-12x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>3x</m> and <m>-8x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>4x</m> and <m>-6x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          Then, look through the list to see which pair adds up to <m>-10x</m>.
          These are <m>2x</m> and <m>-12x</m>, so we can fill in the area model figure as follows:
          <figure>
            <caption>Completed area model figure</caption>
            <image width="25%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                    \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                    \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                    \node at (1,2.25) {$x^2$};
                    \node at (3,0.75) {$-24$};
                    \node at (3, 2.25) {$-12x$}; % upper right area
                    \node at (1,0.75) {$2x$}; % lower left area
                    %
                    \node at (-.5,2.25) {$x$};
                    \node at (1,3.25) {$x$};
                    \node at (-.5,0.75) {$2$}; % 2nd vertical side
                    \node at (3,3.25) {$-12$}; % 2nd horizontal side
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          Thus <m>x^2-10x-24= (x+2)(x-12)</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>
    <p>
      Notice that both techniques involve finding two numbers that multiply to <m>-24</m> and add to <m>-10</m>,
      though in the geometric solution, we were looking for two expressions that multiply to <m>-24x^2</m> and add to <m>-10x</m>.
    </p>


  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-special-cases">
    <title>Special cases</title>

    <p>
      We will look at a continuation of what we saw before, but there are some problems which may be viewed as their own special cases, or can just fit into the general theory we just saw -- your choice!
    </p>

    <!--
    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>x^2+10x+25</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We are looking for two numbers whose product is <m>25</m> and whose sum is <m>10</m>.
          These numbers are <m>5</m> and <m>5</m>. The factorization is 
          <me>(x+5)(x+5).</me>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>
    <p>The special situation here is that the two numbers we found happen to be the same number twice.</p>
  -->

    <principle xml:id="principle-perfect-square-trinomial-plus">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>a^2 + 2ab + b^2 = (a+b)^2</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </principle>
    <p>We can check that this formula is true by taking <m>(a+b)^2</m> and recalling what squaring means:
      <me>(a+b)^2 = (a+b)(a+b) = a^2 + ab + ba + b^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2.</me>
      We note that earlier in <xref ref="warning-exponents-dont-distribute" />, we were told <m>(a+b)^2</m> doesn't turn into <m>a^2+b^2</m> (just with different variables).
      While that earlier warning focused on what we <emph>can't</emph> do, the formula just shown focuses on what we <emph>can</emph> do in this situation.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="factor-xsquaredplus6xplus9">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>x^2 + 6x + 9</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          To apply <xref ref="principle-perfect-square-trinomial-plus" />, we identify <m>a=x</m> and <m>b=3</m>.
          Then,
          <me>x^2 + 6x + 9 = (x+3)^2.</me>
        </p>
      </answer>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We are looking to fill in
          <figure>
            <caption>Initial diamond figure</caption>
            <image width="15%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (-1,-1) -- (1,1);
                    \draw (-1,1) -- (1,-1);
                    \node at (0,.8) {$9$};
                    \node at (0,-.8) {$6$};
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          The two numbers must multiply to <m>9</m> and add to <m>6</m>.
          We list the pairs of numbers whose product is <m>9</m>:
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>1</m> and <m>9</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>3</m> and <m>3</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          Then, look through the list to see which pair of numbers adds up to <m>6</m>.
          These are <m>3</m> and <m>3</m>, so we can fill in the diamond figure as follows:
          <figure>
            <caption>Completed diamond figure</caption>
            <image width="15%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (-1,-1) -- (1,1);
                    \draw (-1,1) -- (1,-1);
                    \node at (0,.8) {$9$};
                    \node at (0,-.8) {$6$};
                    \node at (-.8, 0) {$3$};
                    \node at (.8, 0) {$3$};
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          Thus <m>x^2+6x+9= (x+3)(x+3)</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We are looking to fill in the two empty sides in:
          <figure>
            <caption>Initial area model figure</caption>
            <image width="25%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                    \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                    \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                    \node at (1,2.25) {$x^2$};
                    \node at (3,0.75) {$9$};
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          We consider the product of the first term and last term, <m>9 \cdot x^2</m>. 
          Let's list all pairs of factors of <m>9x^2</m> where each factor contains an <m>x</m>:
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>1x</m> and <m>9x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>3x</m> and <m>3x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          Then, look through the list to see which pair adds up to <m>6x</m>.
          These are <m>3x</m> and <m>3x</m>, so we can fill in the area model figure as follows:
          <figure>
            <caption>Completed area model figure</caption>
            <image width="25%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                    \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                    \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                    \node at (1,2.25) {$x^2$};
                    \node at (3,0.75) {$9$};
                    \node at (3, 2.25) {$3x$}; % upper right area
                    \node at (1,0.75) {$3x$}; % lower left area
                    %
                    \node at (-.5,2.25) {$x$};
                    \node at (1,3.25) {$x$};
                    \node at (-.5,0.75) {$3$}; % 2nd vertical side
                    \node at (3,3.25) {$3$}; % 2nd horizontal side
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          Thus <m>x^2+6x+9= (x+3)(x+3)</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>
    <p>
      Notice that both techniques involve finding two numbers that multiply to <m>24</m> and add to <m>11</m>,
      though in the geometric solution, we were looking for two expressions that multiply to <m>24x^2</m> and add to <m>11x</m>.
    </p>


    <principle xml:id="principle-perfect-square-trinomial-minus">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>a^2 - 2ab + b^2 = (a-b)^2</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </principle>
    <p>We can check that this formula is true by taking <m>(a-b)^2</m> and recalling what squaring means:
      <me>(a-b)^2 = (a-b)(a-b) = a^2 - ab - ba + b^2 = a^2 - 2ab + b^2.</me>
    </p>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>x^2 - 12x + 36</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          To apply <xref ref="principle-perfect-square-trinomial-minus" />, use <m>a=x</m> and <m>b=6</m>.
          Then,
          <me>x^2 - 12x + 36 = (x-6)^2.</me>
        </p>
      </answer>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We are looking to fill in:
          <figure>
            <caption>Initial diamond figure</caption>
            <image width="15%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (-1,-1) -- (1,1);
                    \draw (-1,1) -- (1,-1);
                    \node at (0,.8) {$36$};
                    \node at (0,-.8) {$-12$};
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          The two numbers must multiply to <m>36</m> and add to <m>-12</m>.
          Because the product must be positive, the two numbers we look for must have the same sign.
          Since the numbers we look for must add to <m>-12</m>, both numbers will have negative sign.
          We list the pairs of <emph>negative</emph> numbers whose product is <m>36</m>:
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-1</m> and <m>-36</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-2</m> and <m>-18</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-3</m> and <m>-12</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-4</m> and <m>-9</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-6</m> and <m>-6</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          Then, look through the list to see which pair of numbers adds up to <m>-12</m>.
          These are <m>-6</m> and <m>-6</m>, so we can fill in the diamond figure as follows:
          <figure>
            <caption>Completed diamond figure</caption>
            <image width="15%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (-1,-1) -- (1,1);
                    \draw (-1,1) -- (1,-1);
                    \node at (0,.8) {$36$};
                    \node at (0,-.8) {$-12$};
                    \node at (-.8, 0) {$-6$};
                    \node at (.8, 0) {$-6$};
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          Thus <m>x^2-12x+36 = (x-6)(x-6)</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We are looking to fill in
          <figure>
            <caption>Initial area model figure</caption>
            <image width="25%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                    \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                    \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                    \node at (1,2.25) {$x^2$};
                    \node at (3,0.75) {$36$};
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          We consider the product of the first term and last term, <m>36 \cdot x^2</m>. 
          Let's list all pairs of factors of <m>36x^2</m> where each factor contains an <m>x</m>,
          noting that both coefficients will need to have the same sign (because the product needs to be <m>36x^2</m>)
          and both coefficients will be negative (because the sum needs to be <m>-12x</m>).
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-1x</m> and <m>-36x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-2x</m> and <m>-18x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-3x</m> and <m>-12x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-4x</m> and <m>-9x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-6x</m> and <m>-6x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          Then, look through the list to see which pair adds up to <m>-12x</m>.
          These are <m>-6x</m> and <m>-6x</m>, so we can fill in the area model figure as follows:
          <figure>
            <caption>Completed area model figure</caption>
            <image width="25%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                    \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                    \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                    \node at (1,2.25) {$x^2$};
                    \node at (3,0.75) {$36$};
                    \node at (3, 2.25) {$-6x$}; % upper right area
                    \node at (1,0.75) {$-6x$}; % lower left area
                    %
                    \node at (-.5,2.25) {$x$};
                    \node at (1,3.25) {$x$};
                    \node at (-.5,0.75) {$-6$}; % 2nd vertical side
                    \node at (3,3.25) {$-6$}; % 2nd horizontal side
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          Thus <m>x^2-12x+36= (x-6)(x-6)</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>x^2 - 8x + 16</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          To apply <xref ref="principle-perfect-square-trinomial-minus" />, use <m>a=x</m> and <m>b=4</m>.
          Then,
          <me>x^2 - 8x + 16 = (x-4)^2.</me>
        </p>
      </answer>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We are looking to fill in
          <figure>
            <caption>Initial diamond figure</caption>
            <image width="15%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (-1,-1) -- (1,1);
                    \draw (-1,1) -- (1,-1);
                    \node at (0,.8) {$16$};
                    \node at (0,-.8) {$-8$};
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          The two numbers must multiply to <m>16</m> and add to <m>-8</m>.
          We list the pairs of numbers whose product is <m>16</m>, noting that both numbers will have negative sign (because the sum needs to be <m>-8</m>):
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-1</m> and <m>-16</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-2</m> and <m>-8</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-4</m> and <m>-4</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          Then, look through the list to see which pair of numbers adds up to <m>-8</m>.
          These are <m>-4</m> and <m>-4</m>, so we can fill in the diamond figure as follows:
          <figure>
            <caption>Completed diamond figure</caption>
            <image width="15%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (-1,-1) -- (1,1);
                    \draw (-1,1) -- (1,-1);
                    \node at (0,.8) {$16$};
                    \node at (0,-.8) {$-8$};
                    \node at (-.8, 0) {$-4$};
                    \node at (.8, 0) {$-4$};
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          Thus <m>x^2-8x+16 = (x-4)(x-4)</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We are looking to fill in
          <figure>
            <caption>Initial area model figure</caption>
            <image width="25%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                    \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                    \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                    \node at (1,2.25) {$x^2$};
                    \node at (3,0.75) {$16$};
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          We consider the product of the first term and last term, <m>16 \cdot x^2</m>. 
          Let's list all pairs of factors of <m>16x^2</m> where each factor contains an <m>x</m>, and both factors have negative coefficient:
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-1x</m> and <m>-16x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-2x</m> and <m>-8x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-4x</m> and <m>-4x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          Then, look through the list to see which pair adds up to <m>-8x</m>.
          These are <m>-4x</m> and <m>-4x</m>, so we can fill in the area model figure as follows:
          <figure>
            <caption>Completed area model figure</caption>
            <image width="25%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                    \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                    \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                    \node at (1,2.25) {$x^2$};
                    \node at (3,0.75) {$16$};
                    \node at (3, 2.25) {$-4x$}; % upper right area
                    \node at (1,0.75) {$-4x$}; % lower left area
                    %
                    \node at (-.5,2.25) {$x$};
                    \node at (1,3.25) {$x$};
                    \node at (-.5,0.75) {$-4$}; % 2nd vertical side
                    \node at (3,3.25) {$-4$}; % 2nd horizontal side
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          Thus <m>x^2-8x+16= (x-4)(x-4)</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

    <principle xml:id="principle-difference-of-squares">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>a^2 - b^2 = (a+b)(a-b)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </principle>
    <p>
      To verify this formula, we can expand the right side:
      <me>(a+b)(a-b) = a^2 - ab + ab - b^2 = a^2 - b^2.</me>
    </p>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>x^2 - 49</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We can apply <xref ref="principle-difference-of-squares" /> using <m>a=x</m> and <m>b=7</m>.
          Then,
          <me>x^2 - 49 = (x+7)(x-7).</me>
        </p>
      </answer>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We can write in the missing middle term: <m>x^2-49 = x^2 + 0x - 49</m>, which will be useful for the Diamond problem.
          We are looking to fill in the two empty spaces in:
          <figure>
            <caption>Initial diamond figure</caption>
            <image width="15%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (-1,-1) -- (1,1);
                    \draw (-1,1) -- (1,-1);
                    \node at (0,.8) {$-49$};
                    \node at (0,-.8) {$0$};
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          The two numbers must multiply to <m>-49</m> and add to <m>0</m>.
          Because the product must be negative, the two numbers we look for must have opposite signs.
          We list the pairs of numbers whose product is <m>-49</m>:
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>1</m> and <m>-49</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>7</m> and <m>-7</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>49</m> and <m>-1</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          Then, look through the list to see which pair of numbers adds up to <m>0</m>.
          These are <m>7</m> and <m>-7</m>, so we can fill in the diamond figure as follows:
          <figure>
            <caption>Completed diamond figure</caption>
            <image width="15%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (-1,-1) -- (1,1);
                    \draw (-1,1) -- (1,-1);
                    \node at (0,.8) {$-49$};
                    \node at (0,-.8) {$0$};
                    \node at (-.8, 0) {$7$};
                    \node at (.8, 0) {$-7$};
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          Thus <m>x^2 + 0x - 49 = (x+7)(x-7)</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We are looking to fill in
          <figure>
            <caption>Initial area model figure</caption>
            <image width="25%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                    \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                    \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                    \node at (1,2.25) {$x^2$};
                    \node at (3,0.75) {$-49$};
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          We consider the product of the first term and last term, <m>-49 \cdot x^2</m>. 
          Let's list all pairs of factors of <m>-49x^2</m> where each factor contains an <m>x</m>,
          and due to the product being negative, the coefficients will need to have opposite signs:
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>1x</m> and <m>-49x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>7x</m> and <m>-7x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>49x</m> and <m>-1x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          Then, look through the list to see which pair adds up to <m>0x</m>.
          These are <m>-7x</m> and <m>7x</m>, so we can fill in the area model figure as follows:
          <figure>
            <caption>Completed area model figure</caption>
            <image width="25%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                    \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                    \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                    \node at (1,2.25) {$x^2$};
                    \node at (3,0.75) {$-49$};
                    \node at (3, 2.25) {$-7x$}; % upper right area
                    \node at (1,0.75) {$7x$}; % lower left area
                    %
                    \node at (-.5,2.25) {$x$};
                    \node at (1,3.25) {$x$};
                    \node at (-.5,0.75) {$7$}; % 2nd vertical side
                    \node at (3,3.25) {$-7$}; % 2nd horizontal side
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          Thus <m>x^2 + 0x - 49 = (x+7)(x-7)</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>x^2 - 100</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We can apply <xref ref="principle-difference-of-squares" /> using <m>a=x</m> and <m>b=10</m>.
          Then,
          <me>x^2 - 100 = (x+10)(x-10).</me>
        </p>
      </answer>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We are looking to fill in the two empty spaces in:
          <figure>
            <caption>Initial diamond figure</caption>
            <image width="15%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (-1,-1) -- (1,1);
                    \draw (-1,1) -- (1,-1);
                    \node at (0,.8) {$-100$};
                    \node at (0,-.8) {$0$};
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          The two numbers multiply to a negative, so must have opposite signs. All examples of such pairs of numbers with a product of <m>-100</m> are:
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>1</m> and <m>-100</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>2</m> and <m>-50</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>4</m> and <m>-25</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>5</m> and <m>-20</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>10</m> and <m>-10</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>20</m> and <m>-5</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>25</m> and <m>-4</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>50</m> and <m>-2</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>100</m> and <m>-1</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          Then, look through the list to see which pair of numbers adds up to <m>0</m>.
          These are <m>10</m> and <m>-10</m>, so we can fill in the diamond figure as follows:
          <figure>
            <caption>Completed diamond figure</caption>
            <image width="15%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (-1,-1) -- (1,1);
                    \draw (-1,1) -- (1,-1);
                    \node at (0,.8) {$100$};
                    \node at (0,-.8) {$0$};
                    \node at (-.8, 0) {$10$};
                    \node at (.8, 0) {$-10$};
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          Thus <m>x^2 + 0x - 100 = (x+10)(x-10)</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We are looking to fill in
          <figure>
            <caption>Initial area model figure</caption>
            <image width="25%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                    \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                    \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                    \node at (1,2.25) {$x^2$};
                    \node at (3,0.75) {$-100$};
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          We consider the product of the first term and last term, <m>-100 \cdot x^2</m>. 
          Let's list all pairs of factors of <m>-100x^2</m> where each factor contains an <m>x</m>, and coefficients have opposite signs:
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>1x</m> and <m>-100x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>2x</m> and <m>-50x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>4x</m> and <m>-25x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>5x</m> and <m>-20x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>10x</m> and <m>-10x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>20x</m> and <m>-5x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>25x</m> and <m>-4x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>50x</m> and <m>-2x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>100x</m> and <m>-1x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          The pair which adds to <m>0x</m> is <m>10x</m> and <m>-10x</m>. So
          <figure>
            <caption>Completed area model figure</caption>
            <image width="25%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                    \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                    \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                    \node at (1,2.25) {$x^2$};
                    \node at (3,0.75) {$-100$};
                    \node at (3, 2.25) {$-10x$}; % upper right area
                    \node at (1,0.75) {$10x$}; % lower left area
                    %
                    \node at (-.5,2.25) {$x$};
                    \node at (1,3.25) {$x$};
                    \node at (-.5,0.75) {$10$}; % 2nd vertical side
                    \node at (3,3.25) {$-10$}; % 2nd horizontal side
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          Thus <m>x^2 + 0x - 100 = (x+10)(x-10)</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>

    </example>
    <p>
      Recall that we can always check our factoring by distributing and collecting like terms.
      In fact, practicing this as a habit lets us see why all the strategies we have presented work in the first place,
      and also helps build our intuition to be able to factor in future problems.
      In fact, even in the example we just did, we can check our work by distributing:
      <me>(x+10)(x-10) = x^2 - 10x + 10x - 100 = x^2 - 100.</me>
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-additional-factoring-techniques">
    <title>Additional factoring techniques</title>

    <p>
      We now introduce a strategy that often applies when factoring
      an expression consisting of four terms:
    </p>
    <!-- Strategy -->
    <computation xml:id="factor-by-grouping">
      <title>Factor by grouping</title>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <alert>When does this strategy apply?</alert> 
              This strategy usually (but not always) applies 
              when factoring an expression consisting of four terms.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <alert>How to apply the strategy</alert>
              <ol>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Factor an expression out of the first two terms
                    and factor an expression out of the last two terms.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    In the resulting expression, if both terms have the 
                    same binomial factor,
                    then factor this binomial out of both terms.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ol>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </computation>
    <p>
      Please note that this strategy does not <emph>always</emph> apply.
      However, it <emph>often</emph> does.
      So much so, that whenever there are four terms,
      please consider applying the technique of Factoring by Grouping.
    </p>
    <p>
      In fact, at this point in the course, you have seen a number
      of strategies get introduced.
      Do you ever feel like you get to a quiz or test
      and forget all of the techniques for every style of math problem you were asked?
      You might be already doing this, but if you're not,
      I suggest that you create some index cards,
      one index card for each strategy.
      On the front of an index card, write when the strategy applies.
      (You will note that in each strategy box, the first thing that 
      is mentioned is under the heading <q>When does this strategy apply?</q>)
      On the back of the index card, write how to apply the strategy.
      For example, for the strategy that was just mentioned,
      the front of the index card might say
      <q>This strategy applies 
      when factoring an expression consisting of four terms</q>
      and the back of the card outlines how to apply the strategy.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you've been doing this, and want to add one more layer to
      increase success on quizzes, then may I suggest the following?
      Every time you go through the index cards for review,
      for each index card, come up with a new problem for which the strategy would apply.
      Then, answer the question you created.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="factor-xcubed-plus-5x-squared-plus-2x-plus-10">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>x^3 + 5x^2 + 2x + 10</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          Because we are being asked to factor an expression with four terms,
          we should consider <xref ref="factor-by-grouping" />, 
          the strategy of Factoring by Grouping.
          We first group the first two terms and the last two terms:
          <me>x^3 + 5x^2 + 2x + 10 = (x^3 + 5x^2) + (2x + 10).</me>
          Next, we factor an expression out of each group:
          <me>(x^3 + 5x^2) + (2x + 10) = x^2(x + 5) + 2(x + 5).</me>
          Now, both terms have the same binomial factor, so we can factor this binomial out:
          <me>x^2(x + 5) + 2(x + 5) = (x^2 + 2)(x + 5).</me>
          Putting all of the work together, we have <m>x^3 + 5x^2 + 2x + 10 = x^2(x+5) + 2(x+5) = (x^2+2)(x+5)</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>
    <p>
      As always, we can check our factoring work by distributing:
      <m>(x^2+2)(x+5) = x^3 + 5x^2 + 2x + 10</m>, which we saw in <xref ref="preview-factor-by-grouping" />.
    </p>

    <note>
      <p>
        Before doing another example,
        let's discuss several matters that often arise
        when applying the strategy of Factoring by Grouping.
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Strictly speaking, there is no need to actually
              write parentheses around the two groups of terms.
              That is, from <me>x^3 + 5x^2 + 2x + 10</me>
              we can skip writing <me>(x^3 + 5x^2) + (2x + 10)</me>
              and go directly to <me>x^2(x + 5) + 2(x + 5)</me>
              instead.
            </p>
            <p>
              When going from <me>x^3 + 5x^2 + 2x + 10</me> directly to 
              <me>x^2(x + 5) + 2(x + 5),</me>
              we are essentially factoring by grouping without explicitly 
              writing parentheses to draw the groups.
              Often, an additional question arises when <emph>not</emph> drawing
              in the parentheses: are we allowed to selectively factor like this,
              where we factor the first two terms together and the last two terms together?
              The answer is yes, and the most convincing way to see this is to
              think about what happens backwards.
              If we were given <me>x^2(x + 5) + 2(x + 5)</me> and asked to 
              write this expression differently, we'd distribute to get
              <me>x^3 + 5x^2 + 2x + 10.</me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Once we have <me>x^2(x + 5) + 2(x + 5),</me>
              it's important to not stop here.
              Yes, this step is good, but we should factor completely.
              (In fact, whenever we see the instructions to a math problem 
              is to <q>factor</q>, we should factor <emph>completely</emph>.
              This means that we keep factoring until we can factor no more.)
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              One <emph>extremely</emph> common confusion is how we got 
              from <m>x^2(x+5) + 2(x+5)</m> to <m>(x^2 + 2)(x+5)</m>.
              Why didn't we get <m>(x^2+2)(x+5)^2</m> instead?
              There are four different ways that I wish to address this.
              I hope that you will earnestly read through all four responses:
              <ul>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    First, let us note that in a different problem, if we had <m>y^2u + 2u</m> and we were asked to factor out <m>u</m>,
                    we'd write either <m>u(y^2+2)</m> or <m>(y^2+2)u</m>, but we wouldn't write <m>(y^2+2)u^2</m>.
                    The thing that gets factored out doesn't get an exponent of <m>2</m> attached to it, whether in this example or in our more 
                    complicated example. In fact, the formula we are referencing here is <m>(b+c)a=bc+ca</m>, but note that the left side 
                    is <m>(b+c)a</m> and not <m>(b+c)a^2</m>.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    So, how does this comment apply to our example? In <m>x^2(x+5) + 2(x+5)</m>, the expression we are factoring out is <m>(x+5)</m>, 
                    and this expression <emph>is</emph> admittedly more complicated than factoring out a single variable like <m>u</m>,
                    but it is still true that the thing that gets factored out doesn't get an exponent of <m>2</m> attached to it.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    If you'll continue with me for a second, let's take <m>x^2(x+5) + 2(x+5)</m> and temporarily replace each <m>(x+5)</m> with <m>u</m>,
                    as a bookkeeping device. Then, we have <m>x^2u + 2u</m>, and factoring out <m>u</m> gives <m>u(x^2 + 2)</m> or <m>(x^2+2)u</m>.
                    Now, we said that the replacement was temporary. Since we replaced <m>(x+5)</m> with <m>u</m>, 
                    we now replace <m>u</m> with <m>(x+5)</m> in <m>(x^2+2)u</m> to get <m>(x^2+2)(x+5)</m>.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    As a second explanation, note that we had already checked our factoring by distributing:
                    <m>(x^2+2)(x+5) = x^3 + 5x^2 + 2x + 10</m>.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    As a third explanation, what would happen if we took the expression that I'm claiming is incorrect, namely <m>(x^2+2)(x+5)^2</m>, and distributed?
                    We'd get 
                    <md>
                      <mrow>(x^2+2)(x+5)^2 &amp;= (x^2+2)(x^2 + 10x + 25)</mrow>
                      <mrow>&amp;= x^4 + 10x^3 + 25x^2 + 2x^2 + 20x + 50</mrow>
                      <mrow>&amp;= x^4 + 10x^3 + 27x^2 + 20x + 50</mrow>
                    </md>
                    which is not equal to <m>x^3 + 5x^2 + 2x + 10</m>, the expression we started with.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    As a fourth explanation, we can represent <m>(x^2+2)(x+5)</m> geometrically:
                    <figure>
                      <caption>Geometric interpretation of factoring by grouping</caption>
                      <image width="40%">
                        <latex-image>
                          <![CDATA[
                            \begin{tikzpicture}
                              \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                              \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                              \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                              \node at (1,2.25) {$x^3$};
                              \node at (3,2.25) {$5x^2$};
                              \node at (1,0.75) {$2x$};
                              \node at (3,0.75) {$10$};
                              %
                              \node at (-.5,2.25) {$x^2$};
                              \node at (1,3.25) {$x$};
                              \node at (-.5,0.75) {$2$};
                              \node at (3,3.25) {$5$};
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                          ]]>
                        </latex-image>
                      </image>
                    </figure>
                    The big rectangle has area <m>x^3 + 5x^2 + 2x + 10</m>, by summing the areas of the four non-overlapping small rectangles, which matches the original expression we started with.
                    For a geometric interpretation of what factoring by grouping does, it is putting the four small rectangles into two groups (in this diagram, one group is the two top rectangles, and the other group is the
                    two bottom rectangles).
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ul>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </note>    

    <warning>
      <p>
        We mentioned that certain parentheses can be thrown in optionally, just to visually keep the two groups together. When we do this, we should be careful about exactly where the parentheses go.
        Using our previous question to illustrate, we can turn <m>x^3 + 5x^2 + 2x + 10</m> into <me>(x^3 + 5x^2) + (2x + 10)</me>, but we should not write <me>(x^3 + 5x^2) (+ 2x + 10).</me>
        The difference here is so slight (the second left parenthesis was placed <emph>before</emph> the plus sign), but this changes the meaning. What's the issue here? In writing
        <me>(x^3 + 5x^2) (+ 2x + 10)</me>
        even if there were extra spaces between the two factors:
        <me>(x^3 + 5x^2)\,\,(+ 2x + 10).</me> 
        a missing operation symbol is a hidden multiplication sign.
      </p>
    </warning>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>5x^3 - 10x^2 + 7x - 14</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>(5x^3 - 10x^2) + (7x - 14) = 5x^2(x - 2) + 7(x - 2) = (5x^2 + 7)(x - 2).</m>
        </p>
        <p>
          Note that writing <m>(5x^3 - 10x^2)(+7x - 14)</m> suggests a multiplication that we did not intend.
          Also note the final expression is <m>(5x^2 + 7)(x - 2)</m> instead of <m>(5x^2 + 7)(x - 2)^2</m>.
          To convince yourself this works, let's distribute <m>(5x^2 + 7)(x - 2)</m> to check:
          <m>(5x^2 + 7)(x - 2) = 5x^3 - 10x^2 + 7x - 14</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>2x^3 + 4x^2 + 3x + 6</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>(2x^3 + 4x^2) + (3x + 6) = 2x^2(x + 2) + 3(x + 2) = (2x^2 + 3)(x + 2).</m>
        </p>
        <p>
          As before, we should not write <m>(2x^3 + 4x^2) (+ 3x + 6) </m> as our first expression, and we should not write <m>(2x^2+3)(x+2)^2</m> as our last expression.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

    <p>
      We will need to be slightly more cautious when applying the strategy of Factoring by Grouping if the middle symbol out of the four terms is a minus sign.
    </p>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>4x^3 + 8x^2 - 3x - 6</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>4x^3 + 8x^2 - 3x - 6 = (4x^3 + 8x^2) - (3x + 6) = 4x^2(x + 2) - 3(x + 2) = (4x^2 - 3)(x + 2).</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>4x^3 + 8x^2 - 3x - 6 = 4x^2(x + 2) - 3(x + 2) = (4x^2 - 3)(x + 2).</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>
    <p>
      The second answer skips explicitly writing the parentheses that shows the grouping. To explain what happened:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            From the earlier discussion, we shouldn't write parentheses <m>(4x^3 + 8x^2) (-3x + 6)</m> because this suggests a multiplication that is not actually supposed to be there,
            for exactly the same reason as before. There is a temptation to write <me>(4x^3 + 8x^2) - (3x - 6)</me> instead.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The information about what's going on here is actually an example of <xref ref="sec-distributing-negatives" /> and the examples that followed.
            In <m>4x^3 + 8x^2 - 3x - 6</m>, we factored out <m>4x^2</m> from the first two terms and factored out <m>-3</m> from the last two terms.
            When factoring out <m>-3</m>, to figure out what are the two terms inside parentheses, we ask these questions:
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <emph>What</emph> times <m>-3</m> equals <m>-3x</m>? The answer is positive <m>x</m>, here emphasizng that a positive (what we filled in), multiplied by a negative, results in a negative.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <emph>What</emph> times <m>-3</m> equals <m>-6</m>? The answer is positive <m>2</m>, here emphasizng that a positive (what we filled in), multiplied by a negative, results in a negative.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>4x^3 - 8x^2 - 3x + 6</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>
            4x^3 - 8x^2 - 3x + 6 = (4x^3 - 8x^2) - (3x - 6) = 4x^2(x - 2) - 3(x - 2) = (4x^2 - 3)(x - 2).
          </m>
        </p>
      </answer>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>
            4x^3 - 8x^2 - 3x + 6 = 4x^2(x - 2) - 3(x - 2) = (4x^2 - 3)(x - 2).
          </m>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>p^3-3p^2-4p+12</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>p^3-3p^2-4p+12 = (p^3-3p^2)-(4p-12) = p^2(p-3)-4(p-3) = (p^2-4)(p-3)</m>
          However, our work is not complete, because we can factor <m>p^2-4</m> further:
          <m>(p^2-4)(p-3) = (p+2)(p-2)(p-3)</m>.
          Putting all our work together, we have <m>p^3-3p^2-4p+12 = p^2(p-3)-4(p-3)= (p^2-4)(p-3) = (p+2)(p-2)(p-3)</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

    <p>
      The last example shows an important point: after applying the strategy of Factoring by Grouping,
      we should always check to see if the factors we obtained can be factored further.
      It can also occur that we make our work easier if we factor out a greatets common factor from <emph>all</emph> four terms first, and then apply Factoring by Grouping.
    </p>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>4m^8 - 8m^7 - 60m^5 + 120m^4</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          All four terms have a common factor of <m>4m^4</m>, so we factor this out first:
          <me>4m^8 - 8m^7 - 60m^5 + 120m^4 = 4m^4(m^4 - 2m^3 - 15m + 30).</me>
          Next, we apply Factoring by Grouping to the expression in parentheses, so
          <me>4m^4(m^4 - 2m^3 - 15m + 30) = 4m^4((m^4 - 2m^3) - (15m - 30)) = 4m^4(m^3(m - 2) - 15(m - 2)) = 4m^4(m^3 - 15)(m - 2).</me>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

    <p>
      As part of the process of factoring completely, it is always helpful to see if there is a greatest common factor of all terms first, whether or not Factoring by Grouping follows next.
      This is because factoring out a greatest common factor first can often make the remaining expression easier to factor, because the pieces that we'll see will involve smaller numbers.
    </p>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>x^7 + 10x^6 + 24x^5</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          Because there's three terms instead of four, we won't be using Factoring by Grouping for this question. However, all three terms have a factor of <m>x^5</m>, so we factor this out first:
          <me>x^7 + 10x^6 + 24x^5 = x^5(x^2 + 10x + 24).</me>
          Next, we factor the quadratic expression in parentheses:
          <me>x^5(x^2 + 10x + 24) = x^5(x+4)(x+6).</me>
          Putting all our work together, we have <m>x^7 + 10x^6 + 24x^5 = x^5(x^2 + 10x + 24) = x^5(x+4)(x+6)</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

    <p>
      There are a handful of other special case factoring formulas, but we include these in an appendix.
    </p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-">
    <title>Factoring quadratics revisited</title>

    <!-- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hMFVbo1QKPs -->

    <p>
      So far when we've factored quadratics <m>Ax^2 + Bx + C</m>, we have focused on situations when the quadratic coefficient is <m>A=1</m> as in 
      <m>x^2 + 8x+15</m> or <m>x^2 - 5x - 14</m>. Now we look at situations where <m>A \ne 1</m>, such as <m>4x^2 + 11x + 6</m>.
      We have to slightly modify two of our existing techniques. These are the more general versions of two techniques we saw earlier.
    </p>


    <p>
      First, we generalize the method of factoring quadratics from <xref ref="geometric-factoring-basic" /> that works regardless of the value of <m>A</m>.
    </p>
    <computation xml:id="geometric-factoring-general">
      <p>
        To factor <m>Ax^2+Bx+C</m>, draw a rectangle and divide it into four smaller rectangles.
        Label the top left rectangle with <m>Ax^2</m> and the bottom right rectangle with <m>C</m>.
        In the preliminary version of this method, we labeled two side lengths with <m>x</m>, but we don't do that here.
        <figure>
          <caption>Initial area model figure</caption>
          <image width="25%">
            <latex-image>
              <![CDATA[
                \begin{tikzpicture}
                  \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                  \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                  \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                  \node at (1,2.25) {$x^2$};
                  \node at (3,0.75) {$C$};
                  \node at (3, 2.25) {}; % upper right area
                  \node at (1,0.75) {}; % lower left area
                  %
                  \node at (-.5,2.25) {};
                  \node at (1,3.25) {};
                  \node at (-.5,0.75) {}; % 2nd vertical side
                  \node at (3,3.25) {}; % 2nd horizontal side
                \end{tikzpicture}
              ]]>
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
        Consider the expression <m>(A \cdot C) \cdot x^2</m>, which can also be written <m>ACx^2</m>.
        We need to find two expressions that multiply to <m>ACx^2</m> and add to <m>Bx</m>.
        To do this, it's helpful to first focus on <m>ACx^2</m>. Write a list of pairs of factors that multiply to <m>ACx^2</m> with each factor containing an <m>x</m>.
        In writing the list, note if the two factors will have the same sign or different sign based on the sign of <m>AC</m>.
        Optionally, use the sign of <m>Bx</m> to determine if certain pairs can be left out of the list.
        Then, from this list, find the pair that adds to <m>Bx</m>.
        Once the two expressions are found, place them in the two empty rectangles, since they will represent areas.
        To label the side lengths, in each row and column, find the greatest common factor of the two areas in that row or column.
        Finally, write the factored form using the side lengths, and applying the geometric interpretation of addition (as the gluing together of sticks).
      </p>
    </computation>
    
    <p>
      We generalize the method of factoring quadratics from <xref ref="diamond-method-basic" /> that works regardless of the value of <m>A</m>.
    </p>
    <computation xml:id="diamond-method-general">
      <p>
        To factor <m>Ax^2+Bx+C</m>, draw the following diagram:
        <figure>
          <caption>Initial diamond figure</caption>
          <image width="15%">
            <latex-image>
              <![CDATA[
                \begin{tikzpicture}
                  \draw (-1,-1) -- (1,1);
                  \draw (-1,1) -- (1,-1);
                  \node at (0,.8) {$AC$};
                  \node at (0,-.8) {$B$};
                  \node at (-.8, 0) {};
                  \node at (.8, 0) {};
                \end{tikzpicture}
              ]]>
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
        The remaining two spaces (on the left and right) need to be filled with two numbers that multiply to the number <m>AC</m> at the top of the diamond, and add to <m>B</m> at the bottom of the diamond.
        It is often helpful to write all the factor pairs of <m>AC</m>, and then go through the list to see which pair of numbers adds to <m>B</m>,
        using the signs of <m>AC</m> and <m>B</m> to help narrow down the possibilities.
        Once the two numbers are found, multiply each by <m>x</m>, and then consider the sum of these together with <m>Ax^2</m> and <m>C</m>.
        This will consist of four terms, so we can apply <xref ref="factor-by-grouping" /> to factor the expression completely.
      </p>
    </computation>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>4x^2 + 11x + 6</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          To practice <xref ref="geometric-factoring-general" />, we draw a rectangle that is divided into four smaller rectangles, with the upper left area labeled <m>4x^2</m> and the lower right area labeled <m>6</m>.
          <figure>
            <caption>Initial area diagram</caption>
            <image width="25%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                    \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                    \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                    \node at (1,2.25) {$4x^2$};
                    \node at (3,0.75) {$6$};
                    \node at (3, 2.25) {}; % upper right area
                    \node at (1,0.75) {}; % lower left area
                    %
                    \node at (-.5,2.25) {};
                    \node at (1,3.25) {};
                    \node at (-.5,0.75) {}; % 2nd vertical side
                    \node at (3,3.25) {}; % 2nd horizontal side
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          Next <xref ref="geometric-factoring-general" /> asks us to look at <m>ACx^2</m> which here is <m>(4 \cdot 6)x^2 = 24x^2</m>.
          Let's write pairs of factors that multiply to <m>24x^2</m> where each factor contains an <m>x</m>. In addition, because <m>24x^2</m> has a positive coefficient, both factors must have the same sign.
          The fact that the linear term <m>11x</m> has a positive coefficient means that bost factors must be positive. So, the pairs of factors we have are:
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>1x</m> and <m>24x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>2x</m> and <m>12x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>3x</m> and <m>8x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>4x</m> and <m>6x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          As we mentioned, we didn't include pairs like <m>-2x</m> and <m>-12x</m> because both factors would be negative, which would make their sum negative, which won't help for this problem since we need the sum to be <m>11x</m>.
          In our list of four pairs, we need a pair that adds to <m>11x</m>, and this is the pair <m>3x</m> and <m>8x</m>.
          The two empty areas should be labeled with <m>3x</m> and <m>8x</m> respectively. It doesn't matter which of <m>3x</m> and <m>8x</m> goes in which empty area, but for this example, let's put <m>3x</m> in the lower left area and <m>8x</m> in the upper right area.
          <figure>
            <caption>Second area diagram</caption>
            <image width="25%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                    \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                    \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                    \node at (1,2.25) {$4x^2$};
                    \node at (3,0.75) {$6$};
                    \node at (3, 2.25) {$8x$}; % upper right area
                    \node at (1,0.75) {$3x$}; % lower left area
                    %
                    \node at (-.5,2.25) {};
                    \node at (1,3.25) {};
                    \node at (-.5,0.75) {}; % 2nd vertical side
                    \node at (3,3.25) {}; % 2nd horizontal side
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          Next, we label the side lengths.
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                The upper vertical side is the greatest common factor of <m>4x^2</m> and <m>8x</m>, which is <m>4x</m>.       
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                The lower vertical side is the greatest common factor of <m>3x</m> and <m>6</m>, which is <m>3</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                The left horizontal side is the greatest common factor of <m>4x^2</m> and <m>3x</m>, which is <m>x</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                The right horizontal side is the greatest common factor of <m>8x</m> and <m>6</m>, which is <m>2</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          <figure>
            <caption>Final area diagram</caption>
            <image width="30%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                    \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                    \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                    \node at (1,2.25) {$4x^2$};
                    \node at (3,0.75) {$6$};
                    \node at (3, 2.25) {$8x$}; % upper right area
                    \node at (1,0.75) {$3x$}; % lower left area
                    %
                    \node at (-.5,2.25) {$4x$}; % 2nd vertical side
                    \node at (-.5,0.75) {$3$}; % 2nd vertical side
                    \node at (1,3.25) {$x$};
                    \node at (3,3.25) {$2$}; % 2nd horizontal side
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          Based on the labeled side lengths, we have a geometric representation of <m>(4x+3)(x+2)</m>, and this is our factorization of <m>4x^2 + 11x + 6</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
      <answer>
        <p>
          To apply <xref ref="diamond-method-general" />, we draw the diamond diagram with <m>AC = 4 \cdot 6 = 24</m> at the top and <m>B = 11</m> at the bottom.
          <figure>
            <caption>Initial diamond figure</caption>
            <image width="15%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (-1,-1) -- (1,1);
                    \draw (-1,1) -- (1,-1);
                    \node at (0,.8) {$24$};
                    \node at (0,-.8) {$11$};
                    \node at (-.8, 0) {};
                    \node at (.8, 0) {};
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>`
          </figure>
          We are looking for pairs of numbers that multiply to <m>24</m> and add to <m>11</m>, so both factors need to be positive.
          Here are the pairs of factors of <m>24</m> that are both positive:
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>1</m> and <m>24</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>2</m> and <m>12</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>3</m> and <m>8</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>4</m> and <m>6</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          The pair that adds to <m>11</m> is <m>3</m> and <m>8</m>. So, we consider the expression <m>3x+8x</m>, but we intentionally do not collect like terms yet,
          instead adding this to the original expression's first term <m>4x^2</m> and last term <m>6</m> to get four terms, though for convenience we reordered the terms so that the quadratic term is first,
          the two linear terms in the middle, and the constant term last:
          <me>4x^2 + 3x + 8x + 6.</me>
          Applying Factoring by Grouping,
          <m>4x^2 + 3x + 8x + 6 = (4x^2 + 3x) + (8x + 6) =  x(4x + 3) + 2(4x + 3) = (x + 2)(4x + 3).</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>6x^2 - 13x + 6</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          Our drawing starts with the upper left area labeled <m>6x^2</m> and the lower right area labeled <m>6</m>.
          <figure>
            <caption>Initial area diagram</caption>
            <image width="25%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                    \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                    \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                    \node at (1,2.25) {$6x ^2$};
                    \node at (3,0.75) {$6$};
                    \node at (3, 2.25) {}; % upper right area
                    \node at (1,0.75) {}; % lower left area
                    %
                    \node at (-.5,2.25) {};
                    \node at (1,3.25) {};
                    \node at (-.5,0.75) {}; % 2nd vertical side
                    \node at (3,3.25) {}; % 2nd horizontal side
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          Let's write pairs of factors that multiply to <m>ACx^2 = 36x^2</m> where each factor contains an <m>x</m>. In addition, because <m>36x^2</m> has a positive coefficient, both factors must have the same sign.
          The fact that the linear term <m>-13x</m> has a negative coefficient means that both factors must be negative. So, the pairs of factors we have are:
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-1x</m> and <m>-36x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-2x</m> and <m>-18x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-3x</m> and <m>-12x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-4x</m> and <m>-9x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-6x</m> and <m>-6x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          The pair that adds to <m>-13x</m> is <m>-4x</m> and <m>-9x</m>, so we write these in the empty areas.
          <figure>
            <caption>Second area diagram</caption>
            <image width="25%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                    \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                    \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                    \node at (1,2.25) {$6x^2$};
                    \node at (3,0.75) {$6$};
                    \node at (3, 2.25) {$-4x$}; % upper right area
                    \node at (1,0.75) {$-9x$}; % lower left area
                    %
                    \node at (-.5,2.25) {};
                    \node at (1,3.25) {};
                    \node at (-.5,0.75) {}; % 2nd vertical side
                    \node at (3,3.25) {}; % 2nd horizontal side
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          Next, we label the side lengths.
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                The upper vertical side is the greatest common factor of <m>6x^2</m> and <m>-4x</m>, which is <m>2x</m>.       
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                The lower vertical side is the greatest common factor of <m>-9x</m> and <m>6</m>, which is <m>3</m>, but from the structure of signs in <m>6x^2 - 13x + 6</m>, the side will need to be labeled <m>-3</m>..
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                The left horizontal side is the greatest common factor of <m>6x^2</m> and <m>-9x</m>, which is <m>3x</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                The right horizontal side is the greatest common factor of <m>-4x</m> and <m>6</m>, which is <m>2</m>, but due to the signs, the side will be labeled <m>-2</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          <figure>
            <caption>Final area diagram</caption>
            <image width="30%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                    \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                    \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                    \node at (1,2.25) {$4x^2$};
                    \node at (3,0.75) {$6$};
                    \node at (3, 2.25) {$-4x$}; % upper right area
                    \node at (1,0.75) {$-9x$}; % lower left area
                    %
                    \node at (-.5,2.25) {$2x$}; % 2nd vertical side
                    \node at (-.5,0.75) {$-3$}; % 2nd vertical side
                    \node at (1,3.25) {$3x$};
                    \node at (3,3.25) {$-2$}; % 2nd horizontal side
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          Based on the labeled side lengths, we have a geometric representation of <m>(2x-3)(3x-2)</m>, and this is our factorization of <m>6x^2 - 13x + 6</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
      <answer>
        <p>
          To apply <xref ref="diamond-method-general" />, we draw the diamond diagram with <m>AC = 6 \cdot 6 = 36</m> at the top and <m>B = -13</m> at the bottom.
          <figure>
            <caption>Initial diamond figure</caption>
            <image width="15%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (-1,-1) -- (1,1);
                    \draw (-1,1) -- (1,-1);
                    \node at (0,.8) {$36$};
                    \node at (0,-.8) {$-13$};
                    \node at (-.8, 0) {};
                    \node at (.8, 0) {};
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>`
          </figure>
          We are looking for pairs of numbers that multiply to <m>36</m> and add to <m>-13</m>, so both factors need to be negative.
          Here are the pairs of factors of <m>36</m> that are both negative:
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-1</m> and <m>-36</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-2</m> and <m>-18</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-3</m> and <m>-12</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-4</m> and <m>-9</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-6</m> and <m>-6</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          The pair that adds to <m>-13</m> is <m>-4</m> and <m>-9</m>. So, we consider the expression <m>-4x-9x</m>, but we intentionally do not collect like terms yet,
          instead adding this to the original expression's first term <m>6x^2</m> and last term <m>6</m> to get four terms, though for convenience we reordered the terms so that the quadratic term is first,
          the two linear terms in the middle, and the constant term last:
          <me>6x^2 - 4x - 9x + 6.</me>
          Applying Factoring by Grouping,
          <m>6x^2 - 4x - 9x + 6 = (6x^2 - 4x) + (-9x + 6) = 2x(3x - 2) - 3(3x - 2) = (2x - 3)(3x - 2).</m>
        </p>
      </answer>

    </example>


    <example xml:id="factor-6xsquared-minus-7x-minus-20">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>6x^2-7x-20</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          Our drawing starts with the upper left area labeled <m>6x^2</m> and the lower right area labeled <m>-20</m>.
          <figure>
            <caption>Initial area diagram</caption>
            <image width="25%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                    \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                    \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                    \node at (1,2.25) {$6x ^2$};
                    \node at (3,0.75) {$-20$};
                    \node at (3, 2.25) {}; % upper right area
                    \node at (1,0.75) {}; % lower left area
                    %
                    \node at (-.5,2.25) {};
                    \node at (1,3.25) {};
                    \node at (-.5,0.75) {}; % 2nd vertical side
                    \node at (3,3.25) {}; % 2nd horizontal side
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          Let's write pairs of factors that multiply to <m>ACx^2 = (6)(-20)x^2 = -120x^2</m> where each factor contains an <m>x</m>. 
          In addition, because <m>-120x^2</m> has a negative coefficient, the factors must have opposite signs.
          The fact that the linear term <m>-7x</m> has a negative coefficient means that the bigger coeffcient must be negative.
          So, we don't write the pair <m>-1x</m> with <m>120x</m>. In fact, let's instead start with <m>-120x</m> and <m>1x</m>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-120x</m> and <m>1x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-60x</m> and <m>2x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-40x</m> and <m>3x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-24x</m> and <m>5x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-20x</m> and <m>6x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-15x</m> and <m>8x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>-12x</m> and <m>10x</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          The pair that adds to <m>-13x</m> is <m>-15x</m> and <m>8x</m>, so we write these in the empty areas.
          <figure>
            <caption>Second area diagram</caption>
            <image width="25%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                    \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                    \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                    \node at (1,2.25) {$6x^2$};
                    \node at (3,0.75) {$-20$};
                    \node at (3, 2.25) {$-15x$}; % upper right area
                    \node at (1,0.75) {$8x$}; % lower left area
                    %
                    \node at (-.5,2.25) {};
                    \node at (1,3.25) {};
                    \node at (-.5,0.75) {}; % 2nd vertical side
                    \node at (3,3.25) {}; % 2nd horizontal side
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          Next, we label the side lengths.
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                The upper vertical side is the greatest common factor of <m>6x^2</m> and <m>-15x</m>, which is <m>3x</m>      
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                The lower vertical side is the greatest common factor of <m>8x</m> and <m>-20</m>, which is <m>4</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                The left horizontal side is the greatest common factor of <m>6x^2</m> and <m>8x</m>, which is <m>2x</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                The right horizontal side is the greatest common factor of <m>-15x</m> and <m>-20</m>, which is <m>-5</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          <figure>
            <caption>Final area diagram</caption>
            <image width="30%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                    \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                    \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                    \node at (1,2.25) {$4x^2$};
                    \node at (3,0.75) {$6$};
                    \node at (3, 2.25) {$-4x$}; % upper right area
                    \node at (1,0.75) {$-9x$}; % lower left area
                    %
                    \node at (-.5,2.25) {$3x$}; % 2nd vertical side
                    \node at (-.5,0.75) {$4$}; % 2nd vertical side
                    \node at (1,3.25) {$2x$};
                    \node at (3,3.25) {$-5$}; % 2nd horizontal side
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          One potential confusion is if we should have <m>(3x+4)(2x-5)</m> or <m>(3x-4)(2x+5)</m>.
          To resolve, we can check by expanding. In addition, the placement of minus signs can usually be argued by examining the areas to determine which sides should have a minus sign.
          Based on the labeled side lengths, we have a geometric representation of <m>(3x+4)(2x-5)</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
      <answer>
        <p>
          To apply <xref ref="diamond-method-general" />, we draw the diamond diagram with <m>AC = (6)(-20) = -120</m> at the top and <m>B = -7</m> at the bottom.
          <figure>
            <caption>Initial diamond figure</caption>
            <image width="15%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw (-1,-1) -- (1,1);
                    \draw (-1,1) -- (1,-1);
                    \node at (0,.8) {$-120$};
                    \node at (0,-.8) {$-7$};
                    \node at (-.8, 0) {};
                    \node at (.8, 0) {};
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>`
          </figure>
          We are looking for pairs of numbers that multiply to <m>-120</m> and add to <m>-7</m>, so the factors must have opposite signs, with the number of greater weight being negative.
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>1</m> and <m>-120</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>2</m> and <m>-60</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>3</m> and <m>-40</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>4</m> and <m>-30</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>5</m> and <m>-24</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>6</m> and <m>-20</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>8</m> and <m>-15</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>10</m> and <m>-12</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          The pair that adds to <m>-7</m> is <m>8</m> and <m>-15</m>. We factor
          <me>6x^2 + 8x - 15x - 20.</me>
          Applying Factoring by Grouping,
          <m>6x^2 + 8x - 15x - 20 = (6x^2 + 8x) + (-15x - 20) = 2x(3x + 4) - 5(3x + 4) = (2x - 5)(3x + 4).</m>
        </p>
      </answer>

    </example>
    <p>
      Now that this is our third example, we point out that, in the last answer of the last example, someone may have listed the two numbers <m>8</m> and <m>-15</m> in reverse order, in which case 
      they likely would be looking at factoring
      <me>6x^2 - 15x + 8x - 20.</me>
      This is perfectly acceptable, and when we apply Factoring by Grouping, we get
      <m>6x^2 - 15x + 8x - 20 = (6x^2 - 15x) + (8x - 20) = 3x(2x - 5) + 4(2x - 5) = (3x + 4)(2x - 5),</m>
      which is the same final result as before, just with the two factors in reverse order.
    </p>

  </subsection>


	<subsection>
		<title>Summary</title>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            When asked to factor, be sure to factor completely.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The Diamond Method and the Geometric Method are two methods for factoring quadratic expressions.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            When asked to factor an expression with four terms, please consider using Factoring by Grouping.
            Though this technique is not guaranteed to work, it is often the right tool when there are four terms.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
		<title>Exercises</title>

    <!--
    Construct 25 factoring exercises. Techniques that may be included employ factoring a^2-b^2=(a+b)(a-b) and a^2+2ab+b^2=(a+b)^2 and a^2-2ab+b^2=(a-b)^2. In addition, exercises should include factoring by grouping as well as factoring out the GCF from all terms present, such as x^5 + 2x^4 + x^3. Combining these factoring techniques should be included in the harder questions that appear towards the end. Do not include any problems that require the factoring formulas for a^3+b^3 or for a^3-b^3. In other words, the only way that cubics or higher should appear would be for problems involving Factoring by Grouping or factoring out the GCF from all terms present.
    -->  
    <p>
      <ol>

        <li>
          <p>
            Factor each expression.
            <ol>
<li><p><m>6x + 9</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x^2 + 6x + 9</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x^2 - 16</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>3x^2 + 12x</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x^2 + 10x + 25</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>9x^2 - 25</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x^2 - 2x + 1</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>4x^2 - 9</m></p></li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Factor each quadratic, applying the sign structure method, the diamond method, and the geometric method.
            <ol>
<li><p><m>x^2 + 5x + 6</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x^2 + 7x + 10</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x^2 + 9x + 14</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x^2 + 2x - 15</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x^2 - 3x - 28</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x^2 - 10x + 24</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x^2 + 11x + 18</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x^2 - 5x + 6</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x^2 + 8x + 12</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x^2 - 9x + 20</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x^2 + 3x - 18</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x^2 - 6x - 16</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x^2 + 12x + 35</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x^2 - 7x + 10</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x^2 + 4x - 21</m></p></li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Factor each expression.
            <ol>
<li><p><m>2x^3 + 4x^2</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x^2 - 49</m></p></li>

<li><p><m>3x^2 + 6x + 3</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x^4 - 16</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x^2 + 14x + 49</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>8x^2 - 18</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x^3 + 3x^2 + 2x</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>4x^2 + 12x + 9</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>9x^4 - 4y^4</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>2x^2 + 10x + 12</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x^3 - 2x^2 - 3x + 6</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>a^4 - 81b^4</m></p></li>

<li><p><m>3x^3 + 6x^2 + 2x + 4</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x^4 + 2x^3 + x^2</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>4x^2 - 12x + 9</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>2x^3 - 4x^2 - 3x + 6</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x^5 + 2x^4 + x^3</m></p></li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </li>

      </ol>
    </p>
  </subsection>

</section>